// Practice quiz: Slicing and comprehensions
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/python-fundamentals/slicing-and-comprehensions

::o1 last items::code \= "ABC-1234". Which expression gives "1234"? {
=code[-4\:] #Correct. -4 starts four items from the end, and an empty stop runs to the end.
~code[4\:] #Not quite. Position 4 is the hyphen, so this gives "-1234".
~code[-4\:-1] #Not quite. The stop is excluded, so this gives "123".
~code[\:-4] #Not quite. This takes everything before the last four\: "ABC-".
}

::o1 stop excluded::letters \= ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e"]. What is letters[1\:4]? {
=['b', 'c', 'd'] #Correct. Start 1 is included and stop 4 is excluded.
~['b', 'c', 'd', 'e'] #Not quite. Position 4 ("e") is the stop, and the stop is excluded.
~['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'] #Not quite. Indexes start at 0, so 1 is "b".
~['b', 'c'] #Not quite. That would be letters[1\:3].
}

::o1 step::What is "abcdefg"[1\:\:3]? {
='be' #Correct. Start at index 1 (b), then every third index\: 1 is b, 4 is e, and 7 is past the end.
~'bdf' #Not quite. That uses a step of 2.
~'adg' #Not quite. That starts at 0, not 1.
~IndexError #Not quite. Slices never raise IndexError for bounds past the end.
}

::o1 reverse::Which expression returns a reversed copy of the list nums, leaving nums unchanged? {
=nums[\:\:-1] #Correct. A step of -1 with no start or stop walks the whole list backwards, as a new list.
~nums[-1] #Not quite. That is only the last item.
~nums.reverse() #Not quite. reverse() changes nums in place and returns None.
~nums[-1\:0] #Not quite. With the default step of 1, start -1 is already past stop 0, so the result is empty.
}

::o2 filter and transform::Which comprehension equals\: out \= [] then for w in words\: if len(w) !\= 0\: out.append(w.upper())? {
=[w.upper() for w in words if len(w) !\= 0] #Correct. The kept value comes first, then the for clause, then the if filter.
~[w.upper() if len(w) !\= 0 for w in words] #Not quite. A filter if goes after the for clause; this is a SyntaxError.
~[for w in words\: w.upper()] #Not quite. A comprehension starts with the value, not with for, and has no colon.
~(w.upper() for w in words) #Not quite. Parentheses make a generator, not a list, and the filter is missing.
}

::o2 dict comprehension::names \= ["ana", "bo"]. Which expression builds the dict mapping each name to its length? {
=\{n\: len(n) for n in names\} #Correct. Curly braces with key\: value build a dict.
~[n\: len(n) for n in names] #Not quite. key\: value inside square brackets is a SyntaxError.
~\{len(n) for n in names\} #Not quite. This builds a set of lengths, without the names.
~dict[n, len(n) for n in names] #Not quite. This is not valid comprehension syntax.
}

::o2 set comprehension::What is \{x % 3 for x in [3, 4, 6, 7]\}? {
=\{0, 1\} #Correct. The remainders are 0, 1, 0, 1, and a set keeps each value once.
~[0, 1, 0, 1] #Not quite. Curly braces build a set, not a list, so duplicates are dropped.
~\{0, 1, 0, 1\} #Not quite. A set cannot hold duplicates.
~\{3\: 0, 4\: 1\} #Not quite. Without key\: value, curly braces build a set, not a dict.
}

::o3 side effects::You want to delete every temporary file in a list paths. Which is the clearest code? {
=for p in paths\: delete_file(p), as a normal loop #Correct. The point is the action, so a loop states the intent and builds no unused list.
~[delete_file(p) for p in paths] #Not quite. This builds a throw-away list just to call a function.
~\{delete_file(p) for p in paths\} #Not quite. This builds an unused set.
~deleted \= paths[\:\:-1] #Not quite. This only copies the list in reverse; nothing is deleted.
}
